# Resolved inputs for the shared CUDA image's Python environment
# (containerfiles/Dockerfile.cuda, python-base stage).
#
# KernelAgent itself is NOT listed here: the image builds it from the
# pinned upstream source (KERNELAGENT_REV) into a wheel and installs it
# with --no-deps, so its declared dependency set is locked here instead.
#
# Regenerate requirements.lock with (uv records only part of this in the
# lock header — the index flags matter, they put the cu129 index first):
#
#   uv pip compile requirements.in \
#     --index-url https://pypi.org/simple \
#     --extra-index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu129 \
#     --python-version 3.12 --python-platform linux \
#     --index-strategy first-index \
#     -o requirements.lock
#
# uv gives --extra-index-url priority over --index-url (unlike pip), so
# the cu129 index wins for the packages it mirrors (torch, its nvidia-*
# dependencies) while everything else resolves from PyPI.
#
# The committed lock is generated and tested for the image's default
# configuration: CUDA 12.9 (cu129 torch index), Python 3.12, linux
# x86_64. Other CUDA/Python combinations need their own lock, generated
# and validated the same way, or the build fails on the torch pin (the
# +cu129 wheel does not exist on other indexes).
#
# Maintainer lock policy and validation steps: see AGENTS.md.

# torch: the CUDA 12.9 build, available only on the cu129 index
torch

# Profiling and notebook stack
triton
jupyter
jupyterlab-nvidia-nsight
llnl-hatchet
nvtx

# Imported by KernelAgent's NCU profiler at module level; upstream leaves
# it undeclared (gradio pulls it in transitively today) — lock it
# explicitly so it no longer depends on gradio's dependency tree.
pandas

# KernelAgent runtime dependencies (its pyproject [project] dependencies),
# locked here because the wheel installs with --no-deps:
openai
anthropic<1.0
jinja2
python-dotenv
gradio>=5.5.0
requests
numpy
omegaconf
